   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="address">
                    <ab>
                        <address>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_CHE_ZURI_05">Seefeldstrasse 54</name>, parterre, rechts</addrLine>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_CHE_ZURI">Zurich</name> VIII: <name type="geo" key="G_CHE">Switzerland</name>
                            </addrLine> </address>
                    </ab>
                </div>
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>Dear Miss <name type="person" key="weav76">Weaver</name>: Many thanks for <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">your kind express letter</seg>. I enclose corrections for the third chapter of <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">
                            <rs type="work" key="W_APORTRAIT">my novel</rs>
                        </seg> so that it may be sent on at once to be set up. Tomorrow I shall send these for fourth chapter and also the revised <seg xml:id="seg3" type="note-anchor">cuttings</seg> of first and second if they arrive tonight. I beg you not to trouble to make these corrections yourself. It can be done easily by one of your clerks.</p>
                    <p>I should like to know however is the text which <name type="person" key="marsxx">Mr Marshall</name> read and agreed to publish my unabridged text and also are the names of <seg xml:id="seg4" type="note-anchor">both publishers</seg> to be printed on the title-page, as is sometimes done.</p>
                    <p>
                        <seg xml:id="seg5" type="note-anchor">I enclose a slip</seg> which, if you and Mr Marshall approve, might be printed on the page facing the title page.</p>
                    <closer>
                        <salute>In haste and with kind regards</salute>
                        <salute>sincerely yours</salute>
                        <signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">James Joyce</name>
                        </signed> <dateline>
                            <date when="1916-06-04">4 June 1916 (1916)</date>
                        </dateline> </closer>
                </div>
                <div type="appendix">
                    <p>
                        <seg xml:id="seg6" type="note-anchor">By</seg> the Same Writer</p>
                    <p xml:space="preserve">        	____</p> <p>
                        <name type="work" key="W_DUBLINERS">Chamber Music</name>
                    </p>
                    <p xml:space="preserve">        	(Elkin Mathews: London: 1/6)</p>
                    <p>
                        <name type="work" key="W_DUBLINERS">Dubliners</name>
                    </p>
                    <p xml:space="preserve">        	(Grant Richards: London: 3/6)</p>
                    <p xml:space="preserve">        	____</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">Harriet Weaver’s letter to JJ of 31 May 1916 (Firth 160).</note>
                <note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">
                    <name type="work" key="W_APORTRAIT">A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man</name>.</note>
                <note xml:id="note3" target="#seg3" type="commentary">
                    <p>Cuttings from the pages of <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">The Egoist</name> containing installments of <name type="work" key="W_APORTRAIT">A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man</name>. Due to wartime restrictions, all mail entering Britain was subject to examination by the War Office Censorship. Special provision was made for printed material.</p>
                    <p>Fearing prosecution for obscenity, British printers were reluctant to set <name type="work" key="W_APORTRAIT">A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man</name> in its original form. Hence, Ezra Pound suggested to Harriet Weaver in a letter of 17 March 1916 (as he had done earlier to JJ) that, if all printers refuse, “largish blank spaces be left where passages are cut out. Then the excisions can be manifolded (not carbon copies, but another process) by typewriter on good paper, and if necessary I will paste them in myself” (<name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">LII</name> 375, quoting from <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">The Letters of Ezra Pound</name>, 1907-1941, ed. D. D. Paige [New York, 1950; London, 1951], pp. 122-23).</p>
                </note>
                <note xml:id="note4" target="#seg4" type="commentary">John Marshall and The Egoist Ltd.</note>
                <note xml:id="note5" target="#seg5" type="commentary">For a transcription of the enclosure, see text below beginning “By the Same Writer”.</note>
                <note xml:id="note6" target="#seg6" type="commentary">The following text appears on a separate sheet of paper and was sent with the letter.</note>
            </div>
      </body>
   </text>
